 //! closed (`close_all`, as transfers are stopped, ADR 0023) abandons every socket with no close frame: to Hermes that
 //! is a dropped link, which keeps the owner in control, since they may be part-way through a sign-in. Leaving on
 //! purpose hands control back over `/api/ws` first (transport.ts `releaseScreen`).
+//!
+//! RFB bytes reach the webview in batches (`Batcher`, ADR 0052). Hermes sends one WebSocket message per read of Xvnc's
+//! socket, so one page change comes as dozens to hundreds of small messages, and each message the webview is sent costs
+//! it a script run on its main thread, and one IPC fetch more when it is 1 KiB or larger. Bytes that come within a few
+//! milliseconds of the last batch wait for the next one, so a busy screen costs the webview a few hundred deliveries a
+//! second at most, while a lone change goes at once.
 
